```javascript
test("user can log in with valid credentials", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/login");
  await page.getByLabel("Email").fill("user@example.com");
  await page.getByLabel("Password").fill("correct-horse-battery-staple");
  await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Sign in" }).click();

  await expect(page).toHaveURL("/dashboard");
  await expect(page.getByText("Welcome back")).toBeVisible();
});
```

This drives a real browser, through the real login form, against the real (or a real staging) backend — nothing here is mocked. `getByRole` and `getByLabel` select elements the way a user would perceive them (a labeled input, a named button) rather than by brittle CSS selectors or IDs, which matters directly for how much a UI redesign breaks the test — selecting by accessible role/label survives a class-name change; a `.css-4x9k2` selector doesn't.

Without structure, e2e tests accumulate raw selectors (`page.locator("#login-btn")`) scattered across dozens of test files — so a single UI change (renaming an ID, restructuring the login form) means hunting down and fixing every test that touches it.

A **Page Object** wraps one page's structure and interactions behind a single class:

```javascript
class LoginPage {
  constructor(page) { this.page = page; }
  async goto() { await this.page.goto("/login"); }
  async login(email, password) {
    await this.page.getByLabel("Email").fill(email);
    await this.page.getByLabel("Password").fill(password);
    await this.page.getByRole("button", { name: "Sign in" }).click();
  }
}
```

Every test that needs to log in calls `loginPage.login(email, password)` instead of repeating selectors. When the login form's structure changes, exactly one file — the Page Object — needs updating, and every test using it is fixed simultaneously. This is the same design goal as [test doubles](test-doubles-mocking)' "swap at the boundary": isolate the volatile detail (UI structure) behind one seam.
